;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_11741.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>Parshwanath 59 of 108 - Shri Navpallav Parshwanath.</p><p>Main Deity: An idol of Lord Shri Navpallav Parshwanath in white stone, in the padmasana posture, 33 inches tall and 27 inches wide.</p><p>Location: This is at Kampani Faliya in the town of Mangrol, Junagadh district.</p><p>History: Mangrol, formerly known as Mangalpur, is an ancient port town of Saurashtra. In ancient times, there were many Jain temples here, as a large Jain population resided in the town. This temple of Navpallav Parshwanath is very impressive. It is believed that this temple was built by Maharaja Kumarpal. The idol of Navpallav Parshwanath was installed here in Vikram Samvat 1263. On the advice of Acharya Shri Vijaydharma Ghosh Surishwarji, Minister Pethashah had 84 grand Jain temples built at various places, one of which was this magnificent temple in Mangrol.</p><p>There is a story behind the name Navpallav Parshwanath, recorded in &quot;Shri Navpallavbhasa,&quot; composed by Shri Samayasundar Upadhyay: earlier, this idol of Navpallav Parshwanath stood in a temple in Vallabhipur. When Vallabhipur was invaded and destroyed by non-Aryans in the fourth century, these ancient idols were carried away from there to Prabhas Patan, Bhinmal, and other places. This idol of Navpallav Parshwanath was brought to Mangrol. On the way to Mangalpur (Mangrol), two of the idol&#x27;s fingers were broken. In an instant, a miracle occurred - the guardian deities of the Lord, moved by devotion, caused the idol&#x27;s fingers to be instantly restored (navpallavit, &quot;re-sprouted&quot;) the moment the idol arrived in Mangrol. Hence this idol came to be known as Navpallav Parshwanath. It is believed that this idol of Navpallav Parshwanath dates from the era of King Sampratti. Because of the town&#x27;s name at the time, Mangalpura, this idol is also known as &quot;Mangalpura Parshwanath.&quot;</p><p>Other Temples: There are 2 other temples here. The temple of Shri Suparshwanath Bhagwan is also well worth visiting.</p><p>Directions: Kesod railway station is 25 kilometers away. 1 - From Junagadh (Girnar Tirth) to Prabhas Patan is 94 km, 2 - Prabhas Patan to Chorwad 32 km, 3 - Chorwad to Mangrol 18 km, 4 - Mangrol to Bareja Tirth 41 km. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. There is also an upashraya.</p><p>Scripture References: Navpallav Parshwanath is mentioned in &quot;Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;Shashwat Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;Shri Som Saubhagya Kavya,&quot; &quot;365 Shri Parshwanath Naammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Bhatewa Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,&quot; &quot;Navpallav Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; and others. There is a temple of Navpallav Parshwanath in Khambhat, and in Gogunda, Rajasthan. Idols of Navpallav Parshwanath are found at Jiravala Tirth, at the Kareda Parshwanath temple, and at the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.</p><p>Stuti: &quot;O Lord who caused your own fingers to sprout anew, keep this devotion in my eyes; heal me, who lies enchanted, and make me too sprout anew. O Lord of the town of Mangrol, all the world knows you as Jagdish. &#x27;To Shri Navpallav Prabhu Parshwa, with devotion, I offer my vandana.&#x27;&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Arham Shri Navpallav Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Navpallav Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Kampani Faliya, Post: Mangrol, Taluka: Mangrol, District: Junagadh, State: Gujarat-362225, India. Phone: 02878-222795, Mobile No.: 9723704224.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-077\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Chorwadi Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft h